An immediate read after driving the ports in a testbench may observe old values because evaluation and propagation have not yet settled. That is a simulation observation problem, not a reason to insert hardware registers. Likewise, a flattened synthesized netlist may not preserve \texttt{kept} as a named object: it can combine the AND and XOR bit functions into equivalent logic. The boundary behavior is the contract here.

As a second check, tie \texttt{mask} high in a test parent. For all binary values the child now returns \texttt{a} unchanged, leaving only the XOR. A flattened netlist may optimize away that instance altogether. If the instance disappears under this condition, compare the observable result before assuming a binding failure. If its output never influenced \texttt{y} even with a changing mask, check the port associations and connectivity first.
